    descriptionEn: "Acidcore emerged in early 1990s industrial European metropolises, particularly Rotterdam and London, as an explosive fusion between acid house and nascent hardcore techno. The term combines acid from the iconic Roland TB-303 and core denoting hardcore's extreme intensity. This revolutionary genre arose from radical underground raves, reacting against house music's institutionalization. Producers primarily exploited the Roland TB-303 for its characteristic acid basslines, accompanied by Roland TR-808 and TR-909 drum machines pushed to their limits. The genre's harsh, metallic textures created through analog distortion and creative sampling became its signature. Culturally, acidcore embodied European working-class youth rebellion against economic recession, forging a brutal underground sonic identity. This movement profoundly influenced extreme electronic music and industrial rave culture, establishing acidcore as a cornerstone of 1990s European underground resistance, while its aesthetic brutality reflected the era's social tensions and urban decay.",
