    name: "avant-garde jazz",
    descriptionFr: "L'avant-garde jazz émerge au milieu des années 1950 aux États-Unis, principalement à New York et sur la côte Est, avant de rayonner internationalement. Le terme « avant-garde » emprunté au vocabulaire militaire français signifie littéralement « en avant de la garde », désignant les musiciens explorant les frontières sonores du jazz traditionnel. Ce mouvement révolutionnaire naît de la convergence entre le bebop post-Charlie Parker, la musique classique contemporaine d'Arnold Schoenberg, et les tensions sociales de l'époque des droits civiques. Culturellement, l'avant-garde jazz devient le véhicule d'expression de la communauté afro-américaine en lutte, transformant la protestation sociale en innovation artistique radicale, influençant durablement le rock progressif et la musique électronique contemporaine.",
    descriptionEn: "Avant-garde jazz emerged in the mid-1950s in the United States, primarily in New York and the East Coast, before spreading internationally. The term \"avant-garde,\" borrowed from French military terminology meaning \"advance guard,\" reflects the genre's pioneering role in pushing jazz beyond conventional boundaries. This revolutionary movement fused bebop foundations with contemporary classical techniques, free improvisation, and experimental sound exploration.\n\nInstrumentation expanded beyond traditional jazz ensembles to include extended techniques on conventional instruments-saxophone multiphonics, prepared piano strings, and unconventional percussion objects. Musicians employed techniques like circular breathing, microtonal inflections, and collective improvisation.\n\nTraditional chord progressions dissolved into free harmonic exploration, with emphasis on texture, timbre, and spontaneous interplay rather than predetermined song structures.\n\nCulturally, avant-garde jazz paralleled 1960s civil rights movements and countercultural revolution, challenging established aesthetic and social norms. Its legacy influenced contemporary classical music, free improvisation scenes worldwide, and experimental electronic music, establishing new paradigms for creative expression and artistic freedom in modern music.",
