    descriptionEn: "Canadian indie folk crystallized in the 1960s-70s across Montreal and Toronto, weaving Franco-Canadian and Anglo-Saxon folkloric traditions with modern introspective aesthetics. The nomenclature merges \"indie\" (independent), reflecting artistic autonomy, with \"folk,\" denoting orally transmitted popular songs.\n\nBorn from Quebec's chanson à texte movement and North American folk revival, this genre draws from Beat poetry, modal jazz, and Celtic ballads.\n\nIconic figures Joni Mitchell and Leonard Cohen revolutionized singer-songwriter craft by fusing harmonic sophistication with emotional vulnerability, profoundly influencing international music scenes.\n\nThis movement redefined Canadian cultural identity through its distinctive poetic sensibility, challenging American folk hegemony while establishing Canada as a vital creative force. Its legacy persists in contemporary indie folk, emphasizing literary depth and melodic craftsmanship over commercial appeal, cementing Canada's reputation for thoughtful, innovative songwriting.",
