    descriptionEn: "Viral songs emerged between 2005-2010 with the rise of video-sharing platforms, crystallizing an unprecedented phenomenon of musical propagation. The term \"chanson virale\" combines the French \"chanson\" (song) with \"virale\" (viral), describing compositions engineered for rapid digital dissemination across social networks.\n\nOriginating in major metropolitan hubs like Paris, London, and Los Angeles around 2007-2009, this genre fused elements of pop, electronic dance music, and internet culture aesthetics.\n\nProduction emphasizes compressed dynamics, prominent basslines, and repetitive hooks designed for 15-30 second social media clips. Vocal melodies feature memorable, singable phrases with strategic silence gaps encouraging user participation.\n\nCulturally, chanson virale represents the democratization of music distribution, bypassing traditional industry gatekeepers. These compositions prioritized shareability over artistic complexity, reflecting society's shifting attention spans and participatory digital culture. The genre established template formulas for algorithmic optimization, fundamentally altering music production strategies and redefining commercial success metrics in the streaming era.",
