    descriptionEn: "Cumbia funk emerged in the 1980s-90s as a bold fusion between traditional Colombian cumbia and American funk grooves. This hybrid genre found its roots in the working-class neighborhoods of Bogotá and Mexico City, where musicians experimented with ancestral Afro-Caribbean rhythms. The term 'cumbia' derives from the Bantu word 'cumbé', meaning 'dance', while 'funk' evokes the groove music born in New Orleans. Totó la Momposina brings the authenticity of Mompos traditions, while Andrés Landero preserves the vallenato essence. This fusion transcends social barriers, transforming popular celebrations into transgenerational phenomena. Cumbia funk thus becomes a vehicle for modern Latin American identity, reconciling tradition and innovation in a revolutionary musical métissage that resonates across borders and generations.",
