    descriptionEn: "Dark rock crystallized in late 1960s England as psychedelic rock descended into more sinister territories. The term first appeared in British music press around 1969, describing this exploration of occult and mystical themes. Born from post-1968 disillusionment with hippie idealism, the genre drew from Muddy Waters' electric blues and British Gothic folk traditions. The Beatles initiated this evolution with \"Helter Skelter\" (1968), while Led Zeppelin systematized it from their debut album (1969) and The Rolling Stones explored it on \"Their Satanic Majesties Request\". The genre employed minor pentatonic scales with chromatic descents, creating harmonic tension. Culturally, dark rock embodied emerging British counter-culture, profoundly influencing Gothic aesthetics while prefiguring heavy metal's birth. Its impact resonated through literature, fashion, and underground cinema, establishing darkness as a legitimate artistic expression in popular music.",
