    descriptionFr: "Le deep classic garage rock émerge entre 1963-1969, principalement en Angleterre et dans le Midwest américain (Detroit, Cleveland), fusionnant l'énergie brute du garage rock avec la sophistication naissante du rock classique. Le terme « deep » évoque la profondeur compositionnelle qui distingue ce sous-genre du garage rock traditionnel, intégrant des arrangements plus élaborés tout en conservant l'authenticité DIY.\n\nLes techniques de production rudimentaires en studio 4-pistes amplifient l'esthétique « lo-fi » recherchée.\n\nCulturellement, ce mouvement reflète la rébellion juvénile des années 60, anticipant le hard rock et la pop psychédélique. Il incarne l'esprit contre-culturel naissant, influençant durablement l'évolution du rock indépendant et établissant les codes esthétiques du « son garage » moderne.",
    descriptionEn: "Deep classic garage rock emerged between 1963-1969, primarily in England and the American Midwest, fusing raw garage energy with nascent classic rock sophistication. The term \"deep classic\" denotes the genre's exploration of heavier, more introspective territories beyond standard garage rock's simplicity, while maintaining its foundational DIY ethos.\n\nRooted in British Invasion blues-rock and American garage punk, the genre synthesized Chuck Berry's rhythm patterns with Yardbirds-style experimentation and regional American band rawness.\n\nProduction remained deliberately lo-fi, utilizing small studio reverb chambers and analog tape saturation. Songs typically employed modal scales and minor pentatonic structures, creating darker tonalities than mainstream pop.\n\nCulturally, deep classic garage rock embodied working-class youth rebellion and countercultural emergence, bridging early 60s innocence with late 60s psychedelic complexity. It influenced proto-punk development and established templates for alternative rock's raw authenticity aesthetic, proving that sophisticated musical ideas could coexist with garage rock's primal energy and accessibility.",
