    descriptionEn: "Djent emerged in the mid-2000s, primarily in Sweden with Meshuggah as pioneers, before globalizing through internet communities. The term originates from the onomatopoeia describing the percussive sound of palm-muted guitars, popularized by Meshuggah's Fredrik Thordendal. This hybrid genre draws from progressive metal, math rock, and jazz-fusion polyrhythms, crystallizing an ultra-modern sonic aesthetic. The genre's harmonic language blends dissonant intervals with ambient textures, often incorporating clean passages that contrast with aggressive polyrhythmic sections. Culturally, djent embodies metal's digital era: home-studio production, viral distribution, online communities. It represents a generation of technical musicians pushing rhythmic complexity boundaries while maintaining melodic accessibility. This movement has profoundly influenced contemporary metal, spawning countless bedroom producers and redefining heavy music's relationship with technology and global connectivity.",
