    descriptionEn: "Garage rock revival emerged around 2000, primarily between New York and Detroit, breathing new life into 1960s garage rock. The term originates from original bands literally rehearsing in garages, creating a raw, amateur sound. This movement arose as a reaction against 1990s overproduction, drawing from The Sonics, 13th Floor Elevators, and paradoxically early Beatles, Rolling Stones, and Led Zeppelin albums. The signature sound combines fuzzy, distorted guitars with driving bass lines and punchy drums, often recorded with vintage equipment to achieve authentic tape saturation. Culturally, this revival represents rock's return to basics, valuing authenticity against increasing digitalization. It profoundly influenced indie rock and rehabilitated DIY aesthetics, inspiring a generation of musicians to prioritize raw emotion over technical perfection, ultimately reshaping early 2000s alternative music landscape.",
