    descriptionEn: "Irish rock emerged in the 1960s-70s across Dublin, Belfast, and Cork, as Ireland's youth embraced electric guitar culture while maintaining Celtic identity. The term directly combines \"Irish\" (referring to Gaelic cultural heritage) with \"rock\" (the amplified blues-based genre), representing this cultural synthesis.\n\nThe genre fused traditional Irish folk melodies, jigs, and reels with British Invasion energy and American blues-rock foundations.\n\nChord progressions often utilize open tunings and drone notes reminiscent of Celtic harps. Production frequently emphasizes reverb-heavy atmospherics and layered vocal harmonies.\n\nCulturally, Irish rock provided artistic expression during The Troubles, channeling political frustration and national pride. Bands like Thin Lizzy pioneered twin-guitar harmonies, while U2's anthemic stadium sound and The Cranberries' ethereal Celtic-rock fusion achieved global recognition. This genre established Ireland's modern musical identity, proving traditional culture could evolve within contemporary frameworks while maintaining authenticity and emotional resonance.",
