    descriptionEn: "Korean Indie Rock emerged in early 2000s South Korea, primarily in Seoul's Hongdae university district. The term merges 'indie' (independent) with 'rock,' representing a Korean independent music scene distinct from mainstream K-pop. This movement arose from the intersection of Western rock tradition and Asian melodic aesthetics, influenced by 90s Britpop and Japanese alternative rock. Pioneers CNBLUE and FTISLAND popularized this style beyond national borders. Korean Indie Rock represents an artistic alternative to Korea's entertainment industry, emphasizing musical authenticity and personal expression within a rapidly evolving technological society. The genre reflects young Koreans' desire for creative autonomy while maintaining cultural identity, establishing intimate venue cultures and fostering direct artist-audience connections that contrast sharply with the highly produced K-pop spectacle.",
    subgenres: ["Indie Rock", "Indie Pop", "Dream Pop"],
