    descriptionEn: "Korean Metal emerged in mid-1990s South Korea, fusing Western heavy metal's raw power with traditional Korean harmonic sophistication. The term directly combines 'Korean' and 'Metal', reflecting this unique cultural synthesis. Born during post-democratization era (1987), this genre served as rebellious expression for South Korean youth, drawing direct inspiration from pioneers like Black Sabbath, Metallica, and Iron Maiden. Sonic characteristics include complex riffs blending Western pentatonic scales with Korean modes (such as pyeongjo), creating distinctive sonic identity. The sound typically features heavily distorted power chords, intricate guitar harmonies, and dynamic tempo shifts that mirror traditional Korean musical storytelling patterns. Culturally, Korean Metal symbolizes resistance against rigid social conformity, providing youth an outlet against intense academic and professional pressures of modern South Korean society. This genre represents cultural bridge-building, demonstrating how global musical languages can be authentically localized while maintaining universal appeal.",
