    descriptionEn: "Lo-fi house emerged in the early 2010s from underground studios in Brooklyn and London, driven by artists nostalgic for imperfect sonorities. The etymology combines 'lo-fi' (low fidelity) and 'house', literally designating house music with deliberately degraded sound quality. This movement arose as a reaction to EDM's hyper-perfection, drawing from 1980s Chicago house roots and hip-hop beatmakers' cassette aesthetics. The genre features compressed dynamics, analog warmth, and nostalgic vocal snippets processed through vintage Lexicon reverbs. Culturally, it embodies resistance against digital standardization, creating intimate spaces within club culture. Its social impact lies in democratizing music production, proving that beauty sometimes emerges from technical imperfection. Artists like Ross From Friends and DJ Seinfeld have elevated this DIY ethos into a global movement celebrating authenticity over polish.",
