    descriptionEn: "Rock ABC paulista emerged in São Paulo during the 1960s-70s, named after the ABC metropolitan region (Santo André, São Bernardo, São Carlos) where working-class clubs flourished. This unique fusion arose from the meeting of British rock from The Beatles, Rolling Stones, and Led Zeppelin with Afro-Brazilian rhythms and paulista industrial culture. Guitarists integrate blues pentatonic scales with Dorian and Mixolydian modes, characteristic of Brazilian popular music. Culturally, this genre embodies working-class resistance during the military dictatorship (1964-1985), with metaphorical lyrics evading censorship. Clandestine concerts in industrial suburbs forged a rebellious musical identity, blending social protest with youthful hedonism, durably influencing contemporary Brazilian rock. The movement represents a unique synthesis of international rock aesthetics with local socio-political consciousness, creating an authentic Brazilian rock dialect.",
